 "name": "Forest Healing Landscape in Summer and Autumn (PRS-11)",
 "description": "Faithful replication with the paper's own stimuli: the two photographs from Figure 1 of Kim (2026), Int. J. Environ. Res. Public Health 23(4): 425 (CC BY 4.0), showing the same forest-healing landscape with a pond in Korea, in summer and in autumn. As in the paper, each participant sees both photographs in counterbalanced random order, 30 seconds each with a 3-minute rest between, and rates the 11-item Perceived Restorativeness Scale (PRS-11: being away, fascination, coherence, scope; 1–5) after each. The paper (40 adults aged 40–70 living in Gangwon Province) found perceived restorativeness stable across seasons and sexes; its marginal means are embedded in the page. Differences from the original study: (1) online, without eye tracking (the paper also analysed gaze on sky, forest and pond); (2) the paper's semantic-differential impression ratings are not included because its adjective pairs are not printed; (3) PRS-11 wording follows Pasini et al. (2014) as printed in IJERPH 20: 6354; (4) the photographs are the published figure panels (about 800×430 px).",
